('f0f9a214-fa40-59ff-a105-645bd8c1e88f'::uuid,'d61fc0e9-7136-5375-ab68-40132bc8dd3c'::uuid,'v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0',E'**CHAPTER 6**\n\nDRRM FINANCING, RESOURCE MANAGEMENT AND ACCOUNTABILITY\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)**\n\n**AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*Assessment-informed working draft*\n\n*Subject to institutional, financial, legal and audit validation*\n\n**Draft 1.1 \\| 12 November 2026**\n\n**CONTROLLED WORKING DRAFT**\n\n# Document Control\n\n**Table 6.1**\n\n*Document Control*\n\n| **Document attribute**     | **Control information**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n|----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Document title**         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 6                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| **Chapter title**          | DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| **Position in the Manual** | Sixth of ten chapters in the structure adopted on 12 November 2026. It follows Chapter 5 (Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better) and precedes Chapter 7 (Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement). Drafted first as "Chapter 5" (Draft 1.0) under the earlier nine-chapter plan.                                           |\n| **Document status**        | Working draft for technical, institutional, legal and audit validation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| **Document owner**         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| **Technical custodian**    | CDRRMO Head; Research and Planning Division (formulation and adequacy testing); Administration and Training Division (execution, logistics and property)                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| **Reviewing offices**      | City Budget Office; City Accounting Office; City Treasurer''s Office; Bids and Awards Committee (BAC) and its Secretariat; City General Services Office (CGSO); City Social Welfare and Development Office (CSWDO); City Planning and Development Office (CPDO); City Legal Office; Internal Audit Service, where constituted; OCD Region VIII for RA 12287 readings |\n| **Version / date**         | Draft 1.1 / 12 November 2026                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| **Approval authority**     | To be determined under the City document-control and approval arrangements; in MODE, only the CDRRMO Head approves Manual versions                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| **Review cycle**           | At least annually, before the budget call; after a State of Calamity or a State of Imminent Disaster; after each COA Annual Audit Report; when the DBM–COA guidelines on anticipatory action funds are issued                                                                                                                                                       |\n\n## Revision and Approval Record\n\n**Table 6.2**\n\n*Revision and Approval Record*\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    | **Prepared / reviewed by**        | **Approval** |\n|-------------|-------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | 12 Nov 2026 | First assessment-informed working draft, numbered "Chapter 5" (DRRM Financing, Procurement and Resource Accountability). Built from IAT v1.7 rows A3.5, A4.1–A4.9 and A10.5. Ten SOPs (5-01 to 5-10), eleven FIN forms, Annexes 5-A to 5-C.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Project drafting team with CDRRMO | For review   |\n| 1.1         | 12 Nov 2026 | Renumbered as Chapter 6 under the ten-chapter structure and retitled. SOPs renumbered 6-01 to 6-10 and annexes 6-A to 6-C. Adds resource management: prepositioned stocks and warehousing (section 13, SOP 6-11), the DRRM resource inventory and pre-arranged commitments (SOP 6-12), LOG forms and Annex 6-D. Corrects the RA 12287 IRR Rule IV reference (ten sections; the MODE checklist is derived from them) and cites GPPB Circulars 06-2026 and 07-2026 under RA 12009. Design and captions aligned with Chapters 1 to 5. | Project drafting team with CDRRMO | For review   |\n\n> READING NOTE. This chapter creates no power to appropriate, obligate, procure, disburse or dispose of property. Those powers stay with the Sangguniang Panlungsod, the City Mayor as Head of the Procuring Entity (HoPE), the BAC, the City Budget Officer, the City Accountant, the City Treasurer and the CGSO under the Local Government Code, the procurement law and COA rules. The chapter sets out how the CDRRMO prepares, tracks and documents the DRRM share of that work, so that money and supplies reach the right action on time and every peso and every item can be accounted for.\n\n# 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nChapter 4 turns warnings into protective action, and Chapter 5 turns the damage that remains into recovery. Both depend on money, supplies and a lawful way to buy them. A warning that cannot be matched by funds, stock and a ready supplier does not become action; a recovery program without a fund source stays on paper. This chapter is where the City keeps those means ready and accounts for them.\n\nRA 10121 gives the City a dedicated Local DRRM Fund (LDRRMF). RA 12287 now allows part of it to be spent before a hazard strikes. Both depend on plans approved in advance, procedures that the Budget, Accounting, Treasury, BAC and General Services offices recognise, stocks that are counted and rotated, and records that stand up to audit. The chapter applies the systems-thinking and Total Quality Management approach set out in the Introduction (section 7): one chain from plan to proof, with feedback from utilisation, stock counts and audit into the next plan.\n\nThe IAT v1.7 baseline shows a sound base and a few critical gaps (Figure 6.1). Domain A4 (DRRM Financing and Fund Management) has an index of **2.16 (Developing)**, with 3 of 6 Mandatory items compliant. The 5% LDRRMF, the 70/30 split and the Special Trust Fund are in place and approved (A4.1 to A4.3 at 3). Gaps sit where speed and evidence matter most: the anticipatory action funding pathway (A4.6, score 0, High), pre-arranged procurement (A4.5) and supplier commitments (B6.6), fund adequacy (A4.9), and an inventory that reaches beyond City stocks (B6.3) (Table 6.3).\n\n**Figure 6.1**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Scores for Financing and Resource Indicators*\n\n![Figure 1: 1. Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale](/library/chp6v11/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Scores are copied from the A_CDRRMO and B_EOC sheets of IAT v1.7. The A4 domain index is Σ(score × weight) ÷ Σweight over A4.1 to A4.9 = 41 ÷ 19 = 2.16, banded on the unrounded value. A7.5, B6.3 and B6.6 belong to other domains and are shown because this chapter owns their resource procedures. All rows are pending validation at the workshop of 1–4 December 2026.\n\n**Table 6.3**\n\n*Assessment Findings That Shape This Chapter*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (IAT v1.7)**                                                                       | **Score** | **Priority** | **Finding (summarised from the workbook)**                                                                                   |\n|--------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A4.6   | AA funding pathway in the LDRRMFIP (M, w2)                                                     | 0         | High (12.0)  | No AA line and no disbursement guidance; the CY 2026 plan predates the IRR.                                                  |\n| A4.5   | Emergency procurement documented and pre-arranged (M, w2)                                      | 2         | Medium (6.0) | Supplier list not refreshed; framework agreements not confirmed; FGD evidence only.                                          |\n| A4.8   | Clean COA findings on the LDRRMF, or findings addressed (M, w2)                                | 2         | Medium (6.0) | Status of COA findings not shown; FGD evidence only.                                                                         |\n| A4.9   | Fund adequacy tested against the most severe credible scenario (GP, w2)                        | 1         | Medium (6.0) | No test against a Thelma-type flash flood or a Yolanda-strength typhoon.                                                     |\n| B6.6   | Pre-arranged commitments in signed agreements, usable for AA (GP, w2)                          | 1         | Medium (6.0) | No signed agreements listing item, quantity, trigger and contact.                                                            |\n| B6.3   | Resource inventory (City, barangays, partners, private sector) accessible from the EOC (M, w2) | 2         | Medium (6.0) | City equipment inventoried; barangay, partner and supplier resources not included.                                           |\n| A4.4   | Utilisation monitored and reported; target 80% of the current-year 70% (GP, w2)                | 2         | Medium (4.0) | No quarterly utilisation report; large Trust Fund balances carried forward.                                                  |\n| A3.5   | LDRRMFIP prepared, approved and consistent with the LDRRMP (M, w3)                             | 3         | Medium (4.5) | Prepared and adopted; lines not yet tagged to LDRRMP and CDP PPA codes.                                                      |\n| A4.1   | LDRRMF at not less than 5% of estimated regular revenue (M, w3)                                | 3         | Medium (4.5) | CY 2026 computed on the National Tax Allotment only.                                                                         |\n| A4.2   | 70% / 30% allocation observed (M, w3)                                                          | 3         | Medium (4.5) | Observed; the AA sub-allocation is not yet shown.                                                                            |\n| A7.5   | Prepositioned relief stocks with inventory and replenishment triggers (M, w2)                  | 3         | Low (3.0)    | Use shown by Res. No. 06, s. 2025; no inventory with triggers in the document set. Lowered from 4 for lack of a test or AAR. |\n| A4.3   | Unexpended LDRRMF to the Special Trust Fund and used within 5 years (M, w2)                    | 3         | Low (3.0)    | In use; no ledger ageing balances by year of origin.                                                                         |\n| A10.5  | Full Disclosure Policy postings of the LDRRMF (M, w1)                                          | 2         | Low (3.0)    | Postings not verified; FGD evidence only.                                                                                    |\n\n*Note.* Priority is the workbook band of (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 (Mandatory) or × 1.0 (Good Practice): High 9 or more, Medium 4 to 8.9, Low below 4. B6.2 (resource request and tracking, High) is owned by Chapter 4, SOP 4-07; this chapter supplies the inventory and approval controls behind it. Annex 6-A gives the full row set with master requirements and recommended actions.\n\n## 1.1 What the Records Show\n\n-   **Size of the fund.** CDRRMC Res. No. 05, s. 2025 (30 September 2025) approves the CY 2026 5% LDRRMF Budget Program and Investment Plan at **PHP 139,955,741.40**: PHP 97,969,018.98 (70%) for prevention and mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery, and PHP 41,986,722.42 (30%) for the Quick Response Fund (QRF). The resolution computes the 5% on the National Tax Allotment alone, and its body cites "LDRRMF CY 2025".\n\n-   **Trust Fund.** SP Res. No. 2024-088 adopts the Trust Fund–LDRRMF 2024 plan (PHP 79,512,680.20). CDRRMC Res. No. 01, s. 2025 approves the CY 2025 Trust Fund plan (PHP 52,713,712.18). Res. No. 08, s. 2025 reallocates PHP 2.0 million of it to drainage in Barangay South, after the City Accountant advised that CDRRMC approval and SP concurrence are needed.\n\n-   **QRF use and replenishment of stocks.** Res. No. 06, s. 2025 draws PHP 2.0 million from the QRF to buy food and non-food items that augment CSWDO relief for Typhoon Tino.\n\n-   **Supplemental budget.** Res. No. 07, s. 2025 splits a supplemental PHP 493,550.11 on the 30/70 rule.\n\n-   **Plan hooks.** CDP 2024–2030 PPA 3.15 (Risk and forecast-based Financing Support Program), PPA 1.1.7 (food and non-food items prepositioned for imminent threats), PPA 1.17 (updating of the stockpile inventory system) and PPA 1.3 (Disaster Risk Financing Program, including insurance of equipment, buildings and vital assets) give programme homes for the actions in this chapter. The DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 plans seven storage facilities and a digital inventory, and its SWOC analysis notes that access to the QRF is a challenge.\n\n-   **Donations.** SP Res. No. 2024-102 governs the acceptance of donations.\n\n## 1.2 Design Considerations\n\n1.  **Two clocks.** Normal budgeting runs on an annual calendar; emergency spending and issue of stock run in hours. Both must lead to the same records.\n\n2.  **Three funding windows.** Before impact (anticipatory action funds under RA 12287), during and after impact (the QRF under a State of Calamity), and the rest of the year (the 70% component and the Special Trust Fund). Each window has its own legal trigger, and this chapter keeps them apart (Figure 6.3).\n\n3.  **Pre-arrangement is the speed.** Emergency purchases and stock issues are fastest when the stock level, supplier, framework agreement, specification and price reference exist before the event.\n\n4.  **One item, one record.** A food pack, a generator or a rescue boat has one record from purchase to disposal, shared by the property books, the warehouse stock card and the EOC resource list in MODE.\n\n5.  **Evidence by design.** Every SOP ends in a form that an auditor can follow from appropriation to delivery and use.\n\n6.  **No invented thresholds.** Where national guidelines are still pending (NDRRMC operational guidelines on AA triggers; DBM–COA guidelines on AA funds), the chapter states the gap and holds the parameter in a rule that can change without rewriting the procedure.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter sets the City''s procedures for planning, releasing, spending, recording and reporting DRRM funds; for buying what DRRM needs in normal times and in emergencies; for keeping, rotating and issuing prepositioned stocks; for knowing what resources the City, its barangays and its partners can mobilise; and for accounting for money, goods, property and donations entrusted to DRRM.\n\nIntended results:\n\n1.  The LDRRMF is computed on the correct base, split 70/30, and programmed through an LDRRMF Investment Plan (LDRRMFIP) consistent with the LDRRMP, the CDP and the AIP.\n\n2.  An anticipatory action (AA) line of up to 10% of the 70% component is programmed, with Budget, Accounting, Treasury and BAC steps that allow immediate release under a State of Imminent Disaster and observe the tranche rule.\n\n3.  The QRF is used only within its legal trigger and purpose, and its use is reported.\n\n4.  Unexpended balances are tracked by year of origin and used within five years.\n\n5.  Emergency procurement is pre-arranged, and every emergency purchase can show its legal ground.\n\n6.  Prepositioned relief stocks meet levels set from the contingency plan scenarios, are rotated before expiry and are replenished at defined reorder points.\n\n7.  The EOC can see City, barangay, partner and supplier resources in one inventory, with the commitments behind them.\n\n8.  Donations and external assistance are received, recorded, kept, used and disclosed as the donor and the law require.\n\n9.  Utilisation is reported quarterly to the CDRRMC and posted under the Full Disclosure Policy; COA observations on DRRM funds and property are answered and closed.\n\n10. The City knows how far its funds and stocks cover a severe credible event, and what it would draw on beyond them.\n\n# 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\n**In scope:** the LDRRMF (70% component and QRF); the Special Trust Fund (STF) of unexpended LDRRMF; AA funds under RA 12287; DRRM-related donations and grants in cash or in kind; procurement of DRRM goods, works and services; prepositioned relief stocks and DRRM warehouses; the DRRM resource inventory and pre-arranged commitments; DRRM-funded property and its accountability; risk financing and insurance of public assets; financial reporting, disclosure and audit response.\n\n**Table 6.4**\n\n*Subjects Handled in Other Chapters*\n\n| **Subject**                                                                                        | **Where it is handled**                                                     |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Declaring a State of Calamity or a State of Imminent Disaster                                      | Chapter 1 (SOP GOV-04, SOP GOV-05)                                          |\n| Resource requests, dispatch and tracking during an event; prepositioning decisions during an alert | Chapter 4 (section 7.3; SOP 4-07; form EOC-F06)                             |\n| EOC facility, alternate EOC, power and continuity assets                                           | Chapter 2 (section 2.8)                                                     |\n| Plantilla, salaries, hazard pay and responder welfare policy                                       | Chapter 2                                                                   |\n| Contingency plan scenarios that set stock levels                                                   | Chapter 3 (SOP 3-06)                                                        |\n| Recovery programs, PDNA and recovery financing beyond the QRF                                      | Chapter 5                                                                   |\n| After-action reviews, corrective action register and performance reviews                           | Chapter 7                                                                   |\n| Mutual aid agreements with neighbouring LGUs and partnerships with the private sector              | Chapter 8 (this chapter records the resulting commitments in the inventory) |\n\n**Institutional boundaries.** The CDRRMO plans, requests, monitors and documents. It does not appropriate, certify fund availability, approve disbursement, award contracts, keep the books or hold property accountability on its own. Table 6.5 names who holds each legal function.\n\n**Table 6.5**\n\n*Legal Holders of Financial and Property Functions and the CDRRMO Role*\n\n| **Function**                                                           | **Holder (legal)**                                         | **CDRRMO role**                                                                       |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Appropriation, supplemental budget, realignment                        | Sangguniang Panlungsod (SP)                                | Prepares the justification; the CDRRMC endorses                                       |\n| LDRRMFIP approval                                                      | CDRRMC (RA 10121 Sec. 11; JMC 2013-1), adopted by the SP   | Prepares the draft as secretariat                                                     |\n| Certification of availability of appropriation and allotment           | City Budget Officer                                        | Requests and attaches the LDRRMFIP line                                               |\n| Certification of availability of funds; accounting entries; STF ledger | City Accountant                                            | Supplies supporting documents                                                         |\n| Custody of cash, disbursement, cash advances                           | City Treasurer                                             | Requests release; liquidates                                                          |\n| Procurement decisions and award                                        | HoPE (City Mayor) on BAC recommendation (RA 12009)         | Prepares the PPMP, specifications and justification                                   |\n| Property custody, records, physical count and disposal                 | CGSO                                                       | Receives, issues and accounts for DRRM property; keeps the DRRM asset register        |\n| Relief goods custody and distribution                                  | CSWDO (with CGSO for warehousing)                          | Sets stock levels with CSWDO; tracks stock in MODE; coordinates issue through the EOC |\n| Acceptance of donations                                                | City Mayor under SP Res. No. 2024-102 and applicable rules | Logs; coordinates custody and use                                                     |\n| External audit                                                         | Commission on Audit (COA)                                  | Answers observations through the Mayor                                                |\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\nChapter 1 sets the general legal basis of the Manual. Table 6.6 lists the instruments that govern this chapter. The official text governs. Readings from scanned copies are medium confidence until checked against certified copies.\n\n**Table 6.6**\n\n*Instruments Governing DRRM Financing and Resource Management*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                                  | **What it requires**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | **How this chapter applies it**                                                                                                            |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA 10121 Sec. 21                                                                                | LDRRMF of not less than 5% of estimated revenue from regular sources; 30% as QRF, a standby fund for relief and recovery; unexpended LDRRMF accrues to a special trust fund used for DRRM within the next five years, after which it reverts to the general fund.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | SOP 6-01 (FIN-F01); SOP 6-03 (STF ageing); SOP 6-04 (QRF)                                                                                  |\n| RA 10121 Sec. 16 and IRR                                                                        | State of Calamity declared by the SP on the CDRRMC''s recommendation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Trigger for QRF use (SOP 6-04) and one ground for emergency procurement (SOP 6-07)                                                         |\n| NDRRMC–DBM–DILG JMC No. 2013-1 (25 Mar 2013)                                                    | Allocation and use of the LDRRMF; the LDRRMP is the basis for allocation; LDRRMFIP; allowable expenses; QRF use upon a State of Calamity; reporting.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | SOPs 6-01, 6-02, 6-04                                                                                                                      |\n| COA Circular No. 2012-002 (12 Sep 2012)                                                         | Accounting and reporting for the LDRRMF, NDRRMF transfers to LGUs and receipts from other sources, including donations.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | SOPs 6-02, 6-03, 6-08, 6-10. The section on trust-fund transfers is cited without a section number until checked against the COA original. |\n| RA 12287 (12 Sep 2025) and IRR (signed 14 Sep 2026), Rule V                                     | AA line of no more than 10% of the 70% LDRRMF (Sec. 1(b)); other sources: STF balances, partners and donors, ODA, other LGUs; immediate disbursement subject to procurement, budget, accounting and audit rules (Sec. 2); for hydrometeorological and geological hazards, disbursements for interventions more than 3 days before impact up to 30% of the AA allocation, up to 70% within 3 days (Sec. 2); graduation does not apply to grants, donations or other lawful sources; No Regrets Principle; unused LGU AA funds accrue to the STF (Sec. 3). | SOP 6-05; FIN-F06; Annex 6-C                                                                                                               |\n| RA 12287 IRR Rule IV and Rule VI Sec. 1                                                         | Anticipatory actions (ten sections, including pre-arranged agreements with suppliers); report within 45 days of lifting, including AA fund use.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | SOPs 6-05, 6-06, 6-12; step 8 of SOP 6-05 feeds PR-LIFT-REPORT (SOP GOV-05) and Chapter 7                                                  |\n| RA 12009 (New Government Procurement Act) and IRR                                               | Sec. 35(b) negotiated procurement – emergency cases; Sec. 16 framework agreements.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | SOPs 6-06, 6-07                                                                                                                            |\n| GPPB Circular No. 06-2026 (Res. No. 10-2026, 3 Feb 2026)                                        | Documentary requirements for other modes, including negotiated procurement – emergency cases.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | SOP 6-07 step 4                                                                                                                            |\n| GPPB Circular No. 07-2026 (Res. No. 12-2026, 23 Jul 2026)                                       | Guidelines on the use of framework agreements, applicable to LGUs, with templates for the call-off, the framework agreement and the framework agreement list.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            | SOP 6-06; FIN-F08                                                                                                                          |\n| DILG MC No. 2012-141 and later Full Disclosure Policy issuances                                 | LDRRMF utilisation among the documents posted under the Full Disclosure Policy.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | SOP 6-02 step 6                                                                                                                            |\n| NDRRMC Disaster Preparedness Minimum Standards, Vol. 2                                          | Relief warehouse in a low-risk area; minimum standby stock of 500 food packs and 500 hygiene kits per LGU, each good for a family of five for three days; tagging and inventory.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Section 13; SOP 6-11; Annex 6-D. The national minimum is a floor; Ormoc''s levels come from its own scenarios.                              |\n| COA property rules (PAR, ICS, annual physical count, relief from accountability)                | Accountability for government property and supplies.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Section 14; SOPs 6-10, 6-11, 6-12                                                                                                          |\n| RA 656 (Property Insurance Law); GSIS indemnity                                                 | Public property insured with the GSIS.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | SOP 6-09                                                                                                                                   |\n| RA 9729 as amended by RA 10174 (People''s Survival Fund)                                         | Access to the PSF for adaptation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | SOP 6-09                                                                                                                                   |\n| NDRRMC Memo No. 147, s. 2017                                                                    | LDRRMP review; judicious use of the LDRRMF; AIP consistency.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | SOP 6-01 step 2                                                                                                                            |\n| RA 10173 (Data Privacy Act)                                                                     | Personal data of beneficiaries limited to the offices that need it.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | SOPs 6-08 and 6-11 keep distribution lists with the CSWDO; the CDRRMO records aggregates                                                   |\n| Local: SP Res. Nos. 2024-088 and 2024-102; CDRRMC Res. Nos. 01–08, s. 2025; EO 2024-032A Sec. 2 | Current approvals, donations rules and CDRRMC functions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 | Baseline and approvals (Chapter 1, section 4.5)                                                                                            |\n\n> PENDING NATIONAL RULES. NDRRMC operational guidelines on AA triggers and slow-onset timelines; DBM–COA guidelines on AA funds; the NDRRMC AA monitoring and evaluation framework. Until they are issued, this chapter (a) applies the IRR text, (b) treats slow-onset hazards as not graduated but never above the year''s AA allocation (ADR-025 item 4), and (c) asks the City Legal Office and OCD VIII to confirm the readings listed in section 18.\n\n# 5. Systems Thinking and TQM Principles for DRRM Finance and Resources\n\nThe Introduction (section 5.4) found that ad-hoc fixes during events (borrowed supplies, event-by-event resolutions) relieve pressure faster than fundamental solutions and so keep the office improvising. In finance and logistics the fundamental solutions are known: a plan line before the event, a supplier agreement before the season, a stock level before the forecast. This chapter puts them in place and keeps them visible.\n\n-   **One chain from plan to proof** (Figure 6.2). LDRRMP target → LDRRMFIP line → AIP and budget → PPMP and APP → obligation → procurement → delivery → use → report → audit. A break anywhere shows up as unspent money or unsupported spending.\n\n-   **Speed through preparation.** The fastest emergency purchase is the one whose supplier, specification and price were agreed before the event; the fastest relief is the stock already in a safe warehouse.\n\n-   **Separation of duties.** The office that requests is not the office that certifies, pays, awards or holds the property. MODE records each step by position.\n\n-   **Feedback loops.** Quarterly utilisation, the STF ageing ledger, stock counts and COA observations feed the next LDRRMFIP and the next stock plan.\n\n-   **Prevention over correction.** The pre-audit checklist (Annex 6-B) prevents the most common COA findings: non-DRRM charges, QRF use without a declaration, missing utilisation reports, unliquidated advances, expired stocks and incomplete supporting documents.\n\n**Figure 6.2**\n\n*The Chain From Plan to Proof for Every DRRM Peso*\n\n![Figure 2: 5. Systems Thinking and TQM Principles for DRRM Finance and Resources](/library/chp6v11/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. Dark boxes are the ends of the chain: the LDRRMP target that justifies the spending and the COA audit that tests it.\n\n**Figure 6.3**\n\n*Three Funding Windows and Their Legal Triggers*\n\n![Figure 3: 5. Systems Thinking and TQM Principles for DRRM Finance and Resources](/library/chp6v11/fig-03.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter from RA 10121 Sec. 21, JMC 2013-1 and RA 12287 IRR Rule V. A State of Imminent Disaster does not open the QRF, and a State of Calamity does not open the AA line.\n\n# 6. LDRRMF Planning and Allocation\n\n## 6.1 Computing the Fund\n\nThe LDRRMF is not less than 5% of **estimated revenue from regular sources** (RA 10121 Sec. 21). For a city this means the National Tax Allotment share plus regular local revenue (local taxes, fees and charges, and other recurring income), as defined by the Budget Office under DBM rules. It excludes one-time receipts, grants and borrowings.\n\nThe CY 2026 computation used the National Tax Allotment alone (Res. No. 05, s. 2025). The City Budget Office confirms the base each year on form FIN-F01 and keeps the computation with the budget. If the base was understated, the difference is appropriated through a supplemental budget or corrected in the next budget, as the Budget Office and the SP decide.\n\n## 6.2 The 70/30 Split and the AA Line\n\n**Table 6.7**\n\n*Components of the LDRRMF*\n\n| **Component**                                                                  | **Share**                   | **Use**                              | **Trigger**                                       |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| Quick Response Fund                                                            | 30% of the LDRRMF           | Standby fund for relief and recovery | State of Calamity declared by the SP (JMC 2013-1) |\n| Prevention and mitigation, preparedness, response, rehabilitation and recovery | 70% of the LDRRMF           | PPAs in the LDRRMFIP                 | Normal budget execution                           |\n| – of which the anticipatory action line                                        | No more than 10% of the 70% | AA measures under RA 12287           | State of Imminent Disaster in force               |\n\n**Worked example (illustration only, CY 2026 figures).** The 70% component is PHP 97,969,018.98, so the maximum AA line is PHP 9,796,901.90. For a hydrometeorological or geological hazard, at most PHP 2,939,070.57 (30%) may be disbursed for interventions more than 3 days before forecast impact, and up to PHP 6,857,831.33 (70%) for interventions within 3 days (Figure 6.4). The CY 2026 plan has no AA line, so these figures apply only once a plan includes one.\n\n## 6.3 The LDRRMF Investment Plan\n\nThe LDRRMFIP is the annual plan that turns the LDRRMP into funded lines. Each line carries the LDRRMP and CDP PPA code; the thematic area (prevention and mitigation, preparedness, response, rehabilitation and recovery); the fund source (70%, QRF, STF by year of origin, other); the amount; the implementing office; the output and target; the procurement mode; and the quarter of implementation. Four lines are always shown on their own: the **AA line** (A4.6), the **EOC operations and maintenance line** (B7.5), **stock replenishment** for prepositioned relief items (A7.5), and **insurance premiums** for DRRM assets (A4.7).\n\n## 6.4 Placement in the City Budget Calendar\n\n**Table 6.8**\n\n*Chapter 6 Actions in the City Budget Calendar*\n\n| **Period**       | **Budget calendar event**                                                        | **Chapter 6 action**                                                                                                                                                                          |\n|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| January–February | Prior-year closing; COA audit fieldwork                                          | STF ageing update (SOP 6-03); Q4 utilisation report (SOP 6-02); annual physical count of DRRM property and stocks (SOPs 6-10, 6-11)                                                           |\n| March–May        | COA Annual Audit Report                                                          | Audit response plan (SOP 6-10); supplier list, specifications and stock levels refreshed before the typhoon season (SOPs 6-06, 6-11); inventory update with barangays and partners (SOP 6-12) |\n| By mid-year      | Budget call; Local Finance Committee and Local Development Council deliberations | LDRRMFIP drafting (SOP 6-01); adequacy test (SOP 6-09)                                                                                                                                        |\n| July–September   | CDRRMC approval; AIP finalised                                                   | LDRRMFIP approval; PPMP and APP                                                                                                                                                               |\n| October–December | SP enacts the annual budget and adopts the LDRRMFIP                              | Insurance renewal check; framework agreements for the next year; pre-audit check (SOP 6-10)                                                                                                   |\n| Every quarter    | CDRRMC quarterly meeting                                                         | Utilisation, stock and results report; Full Disclosure Policy posting                                                                                                                         |\n\n*Note.* Exact dates follow the Local Budget Calendar and the City Budget Office''s budget call each year.\n\n# 7. Budget Execution, Utilisation and Disclosure\n\nExecution follows the City''s normal controls: allotment, obligation request certified by the Budget Officer, procurement, inspection and acceptance, disbursement voucher certified by the Accountant, and payment by the Treasurer. The CDRRMO keeps each LDRRMFIP line visible through every step.\n\n**Table 6.9**\n\n*Utilisation Measures Computed Each Quarter*\n\n| **Measure**                      | **Formula**                                        | **Use**                                   |\n|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|\n| Obligation rate                  | Obligations ÷ allotment for the year, by component | Shows whether plans are moving            |\n| Disbursement rate                | Disbursements ÷ obligations, by component          | Shows whether deliveries are being paid   |\n| PPA completion                   | Completed PPAs ÷ PPAs due by the quarter           | Shows results, not only spending          |\n| Utilisation of the 70% component | Disbursements ÷ current-year 70% allotment         | IAT A4.4 target: at least 80% by year-end |\n\nPPAs below plan by the end of the third quarter are flagged for realignment, with CDRRMC approval and SP concurrence where the law requires it. The quarterly report (FIN-F03, carried in MODE as BUD-F02) goes to the CDRRMC, is posted under the Full Disclosure Policy and feeds the Chapter 7 reporting calendar.\n\n# 8. Special Trust Fund Management\n\nUnexpended LDRRMF accrues to the STF and may be used only for DRRM within five years from the year it accrued; after that it reverts to the general fund (RA 10121 Sec. 21). The STF ledger (FIN-F04) keeps each balance by **year of origin** and shows its deadline, so the oldest money is programmed first. STF balances are also a lawful AA source under RA 12287 IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b), and they are not subject to the 30%/70% graduation.\n\nProgramming STF balances follows the same approvals as the 2024 and 2025 Trust Fund plans: CDRRMC approval, then SP adoption or concurrence on the City Accountant''s advice (Res. No. 08, s. 2025). A bill to shorten the five-year period has been filed in Congress but is not law and is not applied.\n\n# 9. Quick Response Fund\n\nThe QRF is a standby fund for relief and recovery in areas struck by a disaster. Under JMC 2013-1 it is used when the City is under a **State of Calamity** declared by the SP (Chapter 1, SOP GOV-04). COA has found LGUs using the QRF without that declaration; this chapter does not allow it. A State of Imminent Disaster under RA 12287 does not open the QRF: anticipatory action is funded from the AA line and other lawful sources (A4.6).\n\nUnused QRF at year-end accrues to the STF. The QRF balance is reported in every quarterly report and after each use (FIN-F05). When QRF purchases replenish relief stocks, as in Res. No. 06, s. 2025, the items enter the stock card under SOP 6-11 like any other receipt.\n\n# 10. Anticipatory Action Financing under RA 12287\n\n## 10.1 Sources and Their Rules\n\n**Table 6.10**\n\n*Sources of Anticipatory Action Funds*\n\n| **Source**                                            | **Legal basis**          | **30%/70% graduation**                              | **Approval route**                                                  |\n|-------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| AA line in the LDRRMFIP (no more than 10% of the 70%) | IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b)     | Yes, for hydrometeorological and geological hazards | Released under the State of Imminent Disaster per the LDRRMFIP line |\n| STF balances within their five-year period            | IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b)(i)  | No                                                  | CDRRMC programming; SP concurrence where required                   |\n| Donations, partners, ODA, other LGUs                  | IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b)(ii) | No                                                  | Per donor terms and SP Res. No. 2024-102                            |\n| NDRRM Fund augmentation                               | IRR Rule V Sec. 1(c)     | Per national rules                                  | Request through OCD; approval on NDRRMC recommendation              |\n\n## 10.2 Release Rules\n\n1.  Funds are released only while a State of Imminent Disaster declared by the City Mayor is in force (Chapter 1, SOP GOV-05), and only for AA measures in the approved PDRA. MODE''s AA checklist is derived from the ten sections of IRR Rule IV; it is a working aid, not a list fixed by law.\n\n2.  Disbursement is based on the anticipated extent of damage and disruption in the PDRA (Rule V Sec. 2).\n\n3.  **Tranche rule** (hydrometeorological and geological hazards): disbursements for interventions to be carried out more than 3 days before forecast impact may not exceed 30% of the AA allocation; up to 70% may be disbursed for interventions within 3 days of impact (Figure 6.4).\n\n4.  **Slow-onset hazards** (for example, drought): timelines and rates await the NDRRMC operational guidelines. Until then they are not graduated, but disbursements never exceed the year''s AA allocation (ADR-025 item 4).\n\n5.  **No Regrets Principle.** Goods bought for AA continue to reach the intended beneficiaries even if the hazard does not materialise (Rule V Sec. 3). Their distribution is recorded as for any other relief.\n\n6.  Procurement, budget, accounting and audit rules still apply (Rule V Sec. 2). Where the procurement ground is "time is of the essence", the BAC records it under RA 12009 Sec. 35(b) (SOP 6-07).\n\n7.  Unused AA funds accrue to the STF (Rule V Sec. 3).\n\n8.  Third parties engaged for AA are paid on a *quantum meruit* basis from the AA allocation (Rule V Sec. 3).\n\n**Figure 6.4**\n\n*Anticipatory Action Allocation and the Tranche Rule, CY 2026 Illustration*\n\n![Figure 4: 10.2 Release Rules](/library/chp6v11/fig-04.webp)\n\n*Note.* Computed from CDRRMC Res. No. 05, s. 2025 and RA 12287 IRR Rule V Sec. 2. Illustration only: the CY 2026 LDRRMFIP has no AA line, and the base may change once the Budget Office confirms the regular-revenue computation (A4.1). MODE applies the 30% and 70% limits from the aa_rules table (ADR-025).\n\nMODE applies the tranche and slow-onset rules from the aa_rules table, so a change in national guidelines is a parameter change, not a code change (ADR-025). Each disbursement is recorded against the declaration with its source, amount, purpose, intervention time and obligation reference, and MODE computes the lead time and tranche.\n\n## 10.3 Getting the AA Line into the Plan\n\nThe CY 2026 plan predates the IRR. Two routes are open, for the City to choose:\n\n-   **Amend the current or next-year LDRRMFIP** by CDRRMC resolution and SP action, realigning within the 70% component to create the AA line (precedent: Res. No. 08, s. 2025); or\n\n-   **Include it in the FY 2028 LDRRMFIP** at the next budget call, under CDP PPA 3.15.\n\nEither way, the line names the AA measures it funds (by hazard and sector, from the contingency plans and the PDRA practice in Chapter 4), the implementing offices and the procurement mode for each item. Stocks to be prepositioned on an imminent threat (CDP PPA 1.1.7) are planned under section 13 so that the AA line buys what the warehouse cannot already supply.\n\n# 11. Procurement for DRRM\n\n## 11.1 Normal Procurement\n\nDRRM goods, works and services follow the PPMP, the APP and competitive bidding or the appropriate alternative mode under RA 12009 and its IRR. The CDRRMO prepares the PPMP from the LDRRMFIP and writes the technical specifications; the BAC runs the procurement.\n\n## 11.2 Pre-arranged Supply\n\nBefore each typhoon season and at least once a year, the CDRRMO and the CGSO, with the BAC Secretariat, keep the following ready (SOP 6-06):\n\n-   a list of suppliers for relief goods, fuel, water, heavy equipment rental, transport, medicines and medical supplies, and generator service, with capacity and a contact position (FIN-F08);\n\n-   framework agreements under RA 12009 Sec. 16 and GPPB Circular No. 07-2026 where the BAC finds them suitable, stating item, maximum quantity, unit price or price basis, delivery time, call-off trigger and validity;\n\n-   standard specifications and price references for recurrent emergency items;\n\n-   agreements usable for anticipatory action, as RA 12287 IRR Rule IV contemplates (B6.6).\n\n## 11.3 Emergency Procurement\n\nNegotiated procurement – emergency cases (RA 12009 Sec. 35(b)) is used only when its ground exists: imminent danger to life or property during a State of Calamity, or time is of the essence because of a calamity and immediate action is needed to prevent loss of life or property or to restore vital public services, infrastructure or utilities. The requesting office writes the ground on FIN-F07 before the purchase, and the BAC and the HoPE confirm it under the RA 12009 IRR. Items are limited to what the emergency requires. Documentary requirements follow GPPB Circular No. 06-2026; posting and reporting follow the IRR and GPPB rules.\n\n# 12. Donations and External Assistance\n\nDonations in cash or in kind and external financial assistance are accepted, recorded and used as follows (SOP 6-08):\n\n1.  One intake point per event, staffed by the CSWDO and the CGSO with the EOC Logistics or Finance and Administration desk.\n\n2.  Acceptance follows SP Res. No. 2024-102 and COA rules; a deed of donation or acknowledgement receipt is issued (FIN-F09).\n\n3.  Cash goes to the City Treasurer and is recorded by the City Accountant under COA Circular No. 2012-002 as a receipt from other sources, kept to its stated purpose.\n\n4.  Goods are inspected, valued, recorded and stored by the CGSO (or the CSWDO for relief items), enter the stock card (SOP 6-11), and are issued against distribution records.\n\n5.  Conditions set by the donor are respected; unsuitable goods are declined politely at intake.\n\n6.  Donations used for AA are not subject to the 30%/70% graduation (IRR Rule V Sec. 2).\n\n7.  Receipts, use and balances are disclosed with the quarterly report.\n\nNo personal data of beneficiaries is copied into the donations log; distribution lists stay with the CSWDO (RA 10173).\n\n# 13. Resource Management and Prepositioned Stocks\n\nChapter 4 coordinates resources during an event: it receives requests, sets priorities and tracks each request from "requested" to "demobilized" (SOP 4-07). It can do so only if the resources exist, are known and can be released lawfully. This section sets how the City keeps relief stocks, warehouses and the resource inventory ready between events (Figure 6.5).\n\n## 13.1 Stock Levels From Scenarios\n\nStock levels are set from the contingency plan scenarios (Chapter 3, SOP 3-06), not from habit. For each priority hazard the CSWDO and the CDRRMO take the number of families expected to be displaced in the most probable scenario, the number of days before national or provincial augmentation is expected, and the ration per family, and compute the level for each item (Annex 6-D). The NDRRMC Disaster Preparedness Minimum Standards set a floor of 500 food packs and 500 hygiene kits per LGU, each good for a family of five for three days; for a city of 85 barangays the scenario figure is likely to be higher, and the higher figure applies.\n\n**Table 6.11**\n\n*Parameters That Set Prepositioned Stock Levels*\n\n| **Parameter**                | **Meaning**                                                                                                  | **Source**                                            |\n|------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|\n| Families to be served        | Families expected in evacuation centres or home-based in the most probable scenario, by cluster of barangays | Contingency plans (CP Forms); CDRA exposure data      |\n| Days of self-reliance        | Days the City must cover before augmentation arrives                                                         | Contingency plans; DSWD Field Office VIII agreements  |\n| Ration per family per day    | Content of the food pack and non-food item kits                                                              | CSWDO standard pack; DPMS Vol. 2 minimum              |\n| Target level                 | Families × days × ration, by item and by warehouse                                                           | Annex 6-D; approved by the CDRRMO Head with the CSWDO |\n| Reorder point                | Level at which replenishment starts, allowing for procurement or call-off lead time                          | SOP 6-06 framework agreement delivery time            |\n| Shelf life and rotation date | Expiry or best-before date and the date the item must be issued or replaced                                  | Supplier documents; stock card                        |\n\n*Note.* No fixed target is set in this chapter. The levels are computed and approved each year under SOP 6-11 and recorded in Annex 6-D.\n\n## 13.2 Warehousing, Rotation and Replenishment\n\n-   **Location.** Warehouses stand in low-risk areas (DPMS Vol. 2), checked against the hazard maps in Chapter 3, and are spread so that one flooded road does not cut off all stocks. The 10-Year Plan''s seven planned storage facilities are sited using the same check.\n\n-   **Condition.** Dry, secure, ventilated and pest-controlled; stock on pallets; a fire extinguisher and a posted layout; access controlled by the custodian.\n\n-   **Rotation.** First expiry, first out. Items within the rotation window are issued to regular CSWDO programmes, feeding programmes or drills before they expire, so nothing is wasted.\n\n-   **Replenishment.** When the stock of an item reaches its reorder point, the custodian raises a replenishment request against the stock replenishment line, the AA line (on an imminent threat) or the QRF (under a State of Calamity), whichever window is open.\n\n-   **Count.** A monthly spot count by the custodian and an annual physical count with the CGSO and the COA schedule; variances are explained in writing.\n\n**Figure 6.5**\n\n*Prepositioned Stock Cycle*\n\n![Figure 5: 13.2 Warehousing, Rotation and Replenishment](/library/chp6v11/fig-05.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter. The two shaded boxes are the decisions that keep stocks adequate. The same item carries one record across the stock card and the MODE resource list.\n\n## 13.3 The DRRM Resource Inventory\n\nThe EOC needs one view of what can be mobilised, whoever owns it (B6.3). The DRRM resource inventory therefore covers four holders: City offices; barangays (BDRRMC equipment and stocks); partners (national agencies, neighbouring LGUs under mutual aid, CSOs); and suppliers and private-sector providers under signed commitments (B6.6). City-owned items are copied from the property records; others are recorded with the agreement that commits them, the contact position, the activation trigger and the date last confirmed. An entry that has not been confirmed in the last twelve months is shown as unconfirmed.\n\nIn MODE, the resources table holds each item with category, unit, quantity, owner office, location and serviceability, and resource requests in Chapter 4 link to it. The current schema has no fields for holder type, commitment reference, reorder point or expiry date. Until they are added, these are kept on the LOG forms (section 20) and noted in the resource record; the gap is listed in section 19 for the platform custodian.\n\n## 13.4 Equipment Readiness\n\nResponse equipment and vehicles are inventoried, serviceable and on a preventive maintenance schedule (A8.3). The CDRRMO keeps the schedule and the maintenance log for DRRM equipment; serviceability is updated in the inventory after each check, so the EOC does not count equipment that cannot be used. Equipment out of service for more than one operational period is reported to the CDRRMO Head with the repair plan.\n\n# 14. Property and Asset Accountability\n\nDRRM property bought with the LDRRMF, STF, QRF, AA funds or donations is government property. The CGSO records it, issues it to the end user on a Property Acknowledgement Receipt or Inventory Custodian Slip, and includes it in the annual physical count. The CDRRMO:\n\n-   keeps a DRRM asset register linked to the MODE resource inventory, so the same item has one record;\n\n-   returns or transfers property on reassignment or separation of the accountable person;\n\n-   reports loss or damage at once, with the incident record, for relief from accountability under COA rules;\n\n-   applies the same rules to equipment lent to barangays, with a signed receipt and a return date.\n\nConsumables issued in an emergency (fuel, food packs, non-food items) are supported by issue slips, trip tickets and distribution summaries. The CDRRMO file holds aggregate distribution counts only; named lists stay with the CSWDO.\n\n# 15. Risk Financing and Insurance\n\nThe City layers its risk finance (Figure 6.6): the QRF and the 70% component for frequent, smaller events; the AA line, the STF and pre-arranged supply for imminent events; insurance for damage to public assets; and national funds, contingent credit and partners for catastrophic events. SOP 6-09 tests that layering each year against a severe credible scenario (a Thelma-type flash flood; a Yolanda-strength typhoon) and records the shortfall and the fallback sources.\n\n**Figure 6.6**\n\n*Risk Layering of DRRM Finance*\n\n![Figure 6: 15. Risk Financing and Insurance](/library/chp6v11/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Prepared for this chapter after the UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard, Essential 3, and the Disaster Rehabilitation and Recovery Planning Guide (NEDA, 2020).\n\nThe CDRA reports that only 15% of critical point facilities are insured and no lifeline utility is insured. Under RA 656, public property is insured with the GSIS. CDP PPAs 1.3 and 1.2.3 programme insurance of buildings, equipment and early warning devices. The EOC, evacuation centres, DRRM warehouses and DRRM equipment are listed first.\n\n# 16. Standard Operating Procedures\n\nEach SOP has a control block and a step table. Every step states the actor (a position or office, never a person), the action and decision rule, inputs and outputs, and timing, control and escalation. Form codes are those of section 20. In MODE, each SOP runs as a workflow (a multi-office management cycle) or a protocol (a point-of-action checklist) that carries the same steps.\n\n> PLATFORM NOTE. SOPs 6-01 to 6-10 were seeded on 12 November 2026 under their Draft 1.0 numbers (WF-BUDGET-CYCLE for SOPs 5-01 and 5-02; WF-SOP5-03, PR-QRF-USE, PR-AA-FUNDS, WF-SOP5-06, PR-EMERGENCY-PROCURE, WF-SOP5-08, WF-SOP5-09 and WF-SOP5-10). The control blocks below give the template codes to be used after the Chapter 6 re-seed (WF-SOP6-xx); protocol codes do not change. SOPs 6-11 and 6-12 are new and their templates are proposed. Until the re-seed, the old codes remain valid aliases.\n\n## SOP 6-01 \\| LDRRMF computation and LDRRMFIP formulation, approval and adoption\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning Division, with the City Budget Office. **Purpose:** Compute the LDRRMF on the correct base and turn the LDRRMP into an approved, costed investment plan with the AA, EOC, stock replenishment and insurance lines shown.\n\n**Table 6.12**\n\n*SOP 6-01 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                                            |\n|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-BUDGET-CYCLE (with SOP 6-02)                                                                            |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMC approves the LDRRMFIP; SP appropriates and adopts                                                             |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Research and Planning; City Budget Office for the computation                                                 |\n| **Trigger**                     | The City budget call each year; a supplemental budget; a realignment request                                         |\n| **Forms**                       | FIN-F01; FIN-F02 (MODE BUD-F01); GOV-F03 (CDRRMC resolution)                                                         |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A3.5 (3, Medium); A4.1 (3, Medium); A4.2 (3, Medium); A4.6 (0, High); B7.5 (3, Low); A1.7 (3, Low); C1.2 (1, Part C) |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-08; MR-03; MR-04; MR-16 (A4.6)                                                                                    |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 10121 Sec. 21; JMC 2013-1; RA 12287 IRR Rule V Sec. 1(b); NDRRMC Memo No. 147, s. 2017                            |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 6-02, 6-05, 6-09, 6-11; Chapter 3 (LDRRMP); Chapter 1 (CDRRMC)                                                  |\n\n**Table 6.13**\n\n*SOP 6-01 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Compute</th>\n<th>City Budget Officer with CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Compute 5% of estimated revenue from regular sources (NTA plus regular local revenue); split 70/30; compute the AA ceiling (10% of the 70%).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is the base complete?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Revenue estimates</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> FIN-F01</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 10 working days of the budget call</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Computation signed by the Budget Officer</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Base dispute: Treasurer and Budget Officer reconcile; unresolved to the City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Review results</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Review LDRRMP targets, last year''s utilisation (FIN-F03), STF ageing (FIN-F04), adequacy test (FIN-F10), stock plan (Annex 6-D), COA observations (FIN-F11) and IAT gaps.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is every input dated? Missing data is named, not estimated.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Prior reports</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Review note</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before drafting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Review note filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Collect proposals</th>\n<th>CDRRMO divisions; sector offices</th>\n<th><p>Submit PPAs with LDRRMP and CDP code, output, cost and procurement mode.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Complete proposal?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Office proposals</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Proposal list</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Two weeks</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> One list, dated</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Draft</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Prioritise life-safety and legal items first; tag each line; show the AA, EOC operations, stock replenishment and insurance lines; apply the DRR-CCA screening to capital items (C1.2).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Does every line trace to an LDRRMP PPA and an allowable use?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Proposal list</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> FIN-F02 draft LDRRMFIP</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Per budget calendar</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Lines without a code removed or justified in writing</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Cost and plan procurement</th>\n<th>Administration and Training with BAC Secretariat</th>\n<th><p>Cost each line; prepare PPMP entries; identify items for framework agreements.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Procurement mode fits the item and timing?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft LDRRMFIP</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> PPMP; procurement plan</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Before the CDRRMC meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> BAC Secretariat review</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> BAC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Endorse</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head; LFC and LDC representatives</th>\n<th><p>Present to the Local Finance Committee and Local Development Council; record CDRRMO input.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Endorsed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Draft LDRRMFIP</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Minutes</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Per budget calendar</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Minutes filed in the Vault</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. Approve</th>\n<th>CDRRMC (CDRRMO as secretariat)</th>\n<th><p>Approve the LDRRMFIP by resolution.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Approved, returned or approved with changes? Returned goes to step 4.</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Endorsed draft</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> GOV-F03 resolution</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Quarterly or special meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Resolution numbered and filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>8. Adopt</th>\n<th>SP</th>\n<th><p>Appropriate and adopt the LDRRMFIP with the annual budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Adopted?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> CDRRMC resolution</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> SP resolution or ordinance</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> With the annual budget</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> SP act filed with the LDRRMFIP</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> Secretariat follows up before year-end</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>9. Publish and load</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Load approved lines into MODE (aa_fund_years for the AA line) and post as required.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Lines match the adopted plan?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Adopted plan</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Controlled LDRRMFIP in MODE</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 10 working days of adoption</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Second person checks the load</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** FIN-F01; FIN-F02; PPMP; LFC/LDC minutes; CDRRMC resolution; SP act; controlled LDRRMFIP in MODE.\n\n## SOP 6-02 \\| LDRRMF execution, utilisation monitoring, quarterly reporting and disclosure\n\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Administration and Training Division, with Research and Planning. **Purpose:** Keep each LDRRMFIP line moving from allotment to use, report it quarterly and disclose it.\n\n**Table 6.14**\n\n*SOP 6-02 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-BUDGET-CYCLE (with SOP 6-01)                                                               |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | Budget Officer, Accountant, Treasurer and HoPE within their powers; CDRRMC and SP for realignment       |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | CDRRMO Administration and Training                                                                      |\n| **Trigger**                     | Start of the fiscal year; end of each quarter                                                           |\n| **Forms**                       | FIN-F03 (MODE BUD-F02)                                                                                  |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A4.4 (2, Medium); A10.5 (2, Low); A10.3 (3, Low)                                                        |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-08; MR-07; MR-11                                                                                     |\n| **Policy basis**                | JMC 2013-1; COA Circular No. 2012-002; DILG Full Disclosure Policy issuances, including MC No. 2012-141 |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 6-01, 6-03, 6-10; Chapter 7 (reporting calendar)                                                   |\n\n**Table 6.15**\n\n*SOP 6-02 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Release</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Request allotment and obligation for each line by its planned quarter.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Line due this quarter?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> LDRRMFIP</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Obligation requests</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Per quarter plan</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Request cites the LDRRMFIP line</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Track</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Record obligation, procurement stage, delivery and disbursement per line from Budget and Accounting records.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Any line stalled for more than 30 days?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Budget and Accounting records</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Line tracker</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Monthly</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Tracker dated</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head for stalled lines</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Reconcile</th>\n<th>City Accountant with Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>Reconcile the CDRRMO tracker with the books (LDRRMF, QRF, STF by year).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Variance found?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Tracker; ledgers</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Reconciled figures</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within 15 days after quarter-end</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Variances explained in writing</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> City Accountant</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Report</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Prepare FIN-F03: obligation, disbursement and completion rates by component; QRF and STF balances; stock levels against targets; PPAs at risk; results against the LDRRMP.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Complete?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Reconciled figures</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> FIN-F03</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> For the next CDRRMC quarterly meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Figures match the reconciliation</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Decide</th>\n<th>CDRRMC</th>\n<th><p>Note the report; direct catch-up or realignment.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Is utilisation of the 70% on track for 80%? A third-quarter shortfall triggers a realignment proposal (SOP 6-01 steps 4–8).</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> FIN-F03</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Minutes; resolution</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Quarterly meeting</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Decision recorded</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Disclose</th>\n<th>Administration and Training with the City Full Disclosure Policy focal person</th>\n<th><p>Post the LDRRMF utilisation report as the Full Disclosure Policy requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Posted within the period?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> FIN-F03</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Posting proof (link or photo)</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Within the FDP period</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Proof filed</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head for a missed posting</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>7. File</th>\n<th>Administration and Training</th>\n<th><p>File reports and proofs in the Vault.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Filed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Reports; proofs</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> Vault entries</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>Time:</strong> Same week</p>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> Vault reference on FIN-F03</p>\n<p><strong>Escalate:</strong> CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Line tracker; reconciliation; FIN-F03; CDRRMC minutes; posting proof; Vault entries.\n\n## SOP 6-03 \\| Special Trust Fund ledger, ageing and programming\n\n**Process owner:** City Accountant (ledger) with CDRRMO Research and Planning (programming). **Purpose:** Use every unexpended LDRRMF balance for DRRM within its five-year period.\n\n**Table 6.16**\n\n*SOP 6-03 Control Block*\n\n| **Control element**             | **Entry**                                                                            |\n|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **Type and MODE template**      | Workflow: WF-SOP6-03 (seeded as WF-SOP5-03)                                          |\n| **Decision authority (legal)**  | CDRRMC programmes; SP adopts or concurs                                              |\n| **Process owner (operational)** | City Accountant; CDRRMO Research and Planning                                        |\n| **Trigger**                     | Year-end closing; LDRRMFIP preparation; any STF reallocation request                 |\n| **Forms**                       | FIN-F04                                                                              |\n| **IAT v1.7 indicators**         | A4.3 (3, Low); A4.6 (0, High)                                                        |\n| **Functional MR links**         | MR-08; MR-16                                                                         |\n| **Policy basis**                | RA 10121 Sec. 21; COA Circular No. 2012-002; RA 12287 IRR Rule V Secs. 1(b)(i) and 3 |\n| **Cross-references**            | SOPs 6-01, 6-05                                                                      |\n\n**Table 6.17**\n\n*SOP 6-03 Steps and Controls*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 22%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and decision rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and outputs / records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, control and escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Close the year</th>\n<th>City Accountant</th>\n<th><p>Determine unexpended LDRRMF (70% and QRF) and transfer it to the STF.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> Balance confirmed?</p></th>\n<th><p><strong>In:</strong> Year-end books</p>\n<p><strong>Out:</strong> STF entry</p></th>\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch6v1_v1.1.md (Chapter 6, v1.1 working draft (IAT v1.7); renumbered from Chapter 5 Draft 1.0)','draft')
